WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.610
Wireless networks operate on the IEEE 802.11 standard. They run on one of two frequencies: the 2.4 GHz frequency or the 5 GHz frequency.

00:10.610 --> 00:18.147
Generally, 5 GHz networks are faster but do not carry signals nearly as well as that of the 2.4 GHz frequency signals.

00:18.147 --> 00:23.947
Try setting up a 5 GHz wireless access point behind some walls sometime and you will see what I mean.

00:23.947 --> 00:28.529
The two frequencies are not compatible on the same wireless network, meaning, for example,

00:28.529 --> 00:34.756
that if you try to connect a device that has a wireless network card that only runs on the 2.4 GHz frequency

00:34.756 --> 00:44.812
to a wireless router that only runs on the 5 GHz frequency, you will not be able to do so. Fortunately, most wireless access points run both frequencies.

00:44.812 --> 00:54.954
And, one of the standards, the 802.11 standard, and one of the standards, the 802.11n standard, supports both frequencies.

00:54.954 --> 01:03.781
Make sure you know the different 802.11 standards, there are five of them that you need to know for the exam, the frequencies they run on, and their top speeds.

01:03.781 --> 01:11.703
In other words, this table.  And yes, you may want to pause the video for a moment and take note of these.

